  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Although substantial rainfall is not expected \ntonight, the chance for showers will increase after midnight.\n\nThere have been a few sprinkles through the late morning and early \nafternoon hours, but nothing that would accumulate. Although the \nprimary round of accumulating rainfall will occur Thursday night \ninto Friday, an upper level shortwave is expected to bring some rain \nto the area tonight. While rainfall amounts will not be terribly \nhigh (expect less than a quarter inch), models have trended upward \nwith the chance for rain tonight, especially in the Triad. The \ncoverage of showers should be rather isolated by sunrise on \nThursday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Shallow and isolated convection brings negligible \nrain amounts through Thurs with shower and storm chances maximizing \nahead of and along a cold front on Fri.\n\nMultiple lobes of mid/upper-lvl low pressure will make up a \npositively tilted trough axis stretching from Quebec into the Ohio \nValley and back into the Southern Plains Thurs morning. Several \npieces of vorticity will ripple through the broad cyclonic flow, \nslowly shifting the trough eastward, while additional northern \nstream waves reinforce the backside of the trough through Thurs \nnight. Lack of mid/upper level forcing within broad neutral H5 \nheight tendencies will keep convective coverage/intensity very \nlimited despite increasing afternoon instability as low-lvl theta-e \nadvection results in around 1000 SBCAPE, primarily across the \nPiedmont. Instability maximized in the lowest 3 km and a \nconsiderable amount of dry air in 3-8 km layer on the backside of a \ndeparting upper-lvl wave, will likely keep any afternoon convection \nshallow and limited to a quick burst of light rain and mostly \nisolated sprinkles through the afternoon and early overnight period.\n\nA disturbance in the basal portion of the positively-tilted trough \naxis is expected to swing across the southern Plains and into the OH \nand TN Valley's early Fri morning the pivot across the Appalachians \nand Mid-Atlantic on Fri. Central NC will likely be glanced by 50-60m \nH5 height falls with deeper scale forcing 60-80m height falls and \nDPVA expected to remain to our north across the Mid-Atlantic through \nFri afternoon/evening. A pre-frontal trough is expected to shift \ngenerally eastward across the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas on Fri, \npreceding the cold front which will likely be bottled up against the \nwindward side of the Appalachians until late Fri afternoon and \nevening. \n\nConsiderable cloudiness from stratus and perhaps some fog in the \nCoastal Plain and eastern-NC is expected Fri morning in addition to \nwaves of elevated showers focused around upstream small-scale \ndisturbances with better rain chances across the eastern Piedmont, \nCoastal Plain and Sandhills through the afternoon. Global numerical \nguidance varies quite significantly from the hi-res guidance with \nrainfall totals and there location through Fri evening; the former \npaints greater coverage of beneficial rainfall (1-3\") across the \narea, while the latter favors mostly around or less than 0.5\" for \nmost locations with narrow swaths of enhanced rainfall (0.75-2\") \nalong the track of these upstream disturbances. This forecast \npackage leans more heavily into the hi-res guidance and overall \nlower-end totals for most locations given limited coverage of \ninstability > 1000 J/kg, glancing influences from better deep-layer \nforcing, questions on persistence of low overcast into the afternoon \nhours, and worsening drought over the Carolinas. The severe threat \nremains non-zero with low-predictability for Fri. Upper 60s dew \npoints lifting into the area and overlapping 30-40 kts of 0-6 km \nshear will be favorable for providing instability and organization \nto any deepening convection. Gradually veering wind profiles, mainly \nin the mid/upper-lvls, with a steadily increase in magnitude with \nheight will be favorable for multicell to transient supercell \ncharacteristics, but limited instability and potential storm \ninteractions along the track of upstream disturbances may limit \nwidespread storm longevity and associated severe threat. Primary \nrisk would be localized strong wind gusts (30-50 mph) around and \neast of the I-95 corridor, although infrequent and brief severe wind \ngusts of 60 mph can't be ruled out given the uncertainty expressed \nabove.",
